# Ring firmware application options.
#
# This file becomes KCONFIG_ROOT for the application (see Zephyr's
# cmake/modules/kconfig.cmake), so it MUST end by sourcing Kconfig.zephyr or
# no Zephyr symbol resolves at all.
#
# Everything bench-only lives here rather than as a #define inside a .c
# file. That is deliberate: the flags used to be scattered across main.c and
# imu.c, and "production" was produced by hand-editing main.c and keeping a
# main.c.bench copy alongside it. Two files that have to be edited in step
# is a bug waiting to happen, and the duplicate rotted immediately.
#
# Now both images come from one tree with no file edits:
#
#   .\build.ps1            production
#   .\build.ps1 -Bench     bench instrumentation

mainmenu "Ring firmware"

menu "Ring firmware"

config RING_BENCH
	bool "Bench instrumentation (NOT for anything worn or shipped)"
	help
	  Master switch for every bench-only instrument in this firmware.

	  These instruments perturb what they measure and defeat the power
	  model, which is fine on a desk with a debugger attached and wrong
	  everywhere else. Off by default so an ordinary build is shippable.

if RING_BENCH

config RING_BENCH_POWER_LED
	bool "Hold the green LED on as a power indicator"
	default y
	help
	  Holds MAXM86161 LED1 solid so "is the board actually running?" is
	  answerable by looking at it rather than by attaching a debugger.
	  Hours went into faults that turned out to be an unpowered board
	  presenting as a dead SWD link.

	  Costs real current and keeps the 5 V boost enabled permanently,
	  so the idle power model does not apply while this is set.

config RING_IMU_WAKE_DIAG
	bool "IMU wake-path diagnostics"
	default y
	help
	  Samples the INT1 pin before reading the latched wake source, which
	  separates "the IMU never fired" from "the IMU fired and the pin did
	  not move". This is what localised the cold joint on U4.

	  It reads latched registers and pulses INT1, so it perturbs the very
	  interrupt path the firmware depends on. Never leave it on outside
	  bring-up.

config RING_GSR_MONITOR
	bool "Live GSR monitor"
	default y
	help
	  Samples GSR in every state and holds GSR_PWR on continuously so the
	  front end stays settled and a meter can reach it.

	  Holding GSR_PWR on defeats duty cycling and keeps the analog front
	  end powered for as long as the board is.

endif # RING_BENCH

config RING_WATCHDOG
	bool "Hardware watchdog"
	default y
	select WATCHDOG
	help
	  Resets the SoC if the main loop stops feeding it. Without this a
	  wedged I2C bus or a hung loop is an unrecoverable brick that only a
	  power cycle clears -- and on a sealed ring, that means waiting for
	  the battery to die.

	  The nRF52833 watchdog CANNOT be stopped once started; only a
	  power-on reset clears it. It is therefore started last, after all
	  initialisation has succeeded.

config RING_WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT_MS
	int "Watchdog timeout (ms)"
	default 10000
	range 1000 100000
	depends on RING_WATCHDOG
	help
	  Must comfortably exceed the longest span between two
	  ring_wdt_feed() calls, because exceeding it reboots a board that
	  is merely slow.

	  THE AUDIT LIVES BESIDE THE FEED CALL IN main.c, and that is the
	  only copy. A duplicate of the numbers used to sit here and went
	  stale immediately: it counted only the k_msleep() delays, missed
	  that a stalled I2C bus costs 500 ms per transaction, and claimed
	  a margin that did not exist. Read main.c rather than trusting a
	  figure quoted here.

config RING_SLEEP_WEAR_CHECK
	bool "Corroborate sleep sessions with a PPG wear check"
	default y
	help
	  Opens a short PPG window every so often while a sleep session is
	  open, purely to read the DC level and answer "is this ring on a
	  hand?". Without it, stillness is the only input the sleep tracker
	  has and a ring left on a nightstand logs a full night -- confirmed
	  behaviour, not a hypothesis.

	  It costs LED current at night, but only where the answer is "no".
	  A worn ring never goes stale -- finding a finger during an ordinary
	  window refreshes the motion timeout -- so it is already measuring
	  every minute and its scheduled windows answer this for free: at
	  most one extra 6 s window per session, and usually none. A ring off
	  a finger DOES go stale three minutes in, and there the check adds
	  roughly 16 windows of 6 s, about 96 s of LED1 per night at ~15.4 mA
	  off the 5 V rail, against the ~12 s that night cost before.

	  That is ~0.41 mAh at 5 V, or ~0.65 mAh referred to a 3.7 V cell at
	  an assumed 85 % boost efficiency, before the AFE's own draw. NOBODY
	  HAS MEASURED THE BATTERY, so what fraction of a night that is
	  remains unknown. The arithmetic and the simulated window counts are
	  in STATUS.md section 5; the far larger pre-existing cost of the
	  duty cycle itself is R13 in the same file.

	  The verdict is reported, never enforced: a session that fails the
	  check is still reported as sleep, and the app decides what to do
	  with it.

	  Set this to n and no window is ever opened to ask the question, so
	  the power model is exactly what it was before this option existed.
	  Sessions then report RING_WEAR_UNKNOWN, except where a window
	  opened for some other reason -- an app-forced measurement, opcode
	  0x03 -- happens to land inside one. That reading is free and is
	  still recorded either way.

config RING_SLEEP_WEAR_CHECK_INTERVAL_MIN
	int "Minutes between sleep wear checks"
	default 30
	range 5 1440
	depends on RING_SLEEP_WEAR_CHECK
	help
	  How long to wait between wear checks within one sleep session. The
	  first check of a session is taken as soon as the session opens, so
	  a verdict of "not worn" -- which needs two negatives -- lands about
	  one interval after onset.

	  Cost scales linearly: halving this doubles the LED time per night.
	  Set it beyond a night's length (say 600) to get exactly one check
	  per session, which is the cheapest useful setting and cannot notice
	  a ring taken off at 2 am.

config RING_SLEEP_WEAR_CHECK_WINDOW_MS
	int "Length of a sleep wear-check window (ms)"
	default 6000
	range 4000 30000
	depends on RING_SLEEP_WEAR_CHECK
	help
	  How long the PPG runs for one wear check. This only has to settle
	  the DC tracker, not acquire a heart rate, so it is far shorter than
	  MEASURE_WINDOW_MS.

	  The floor is the ~4 s settling figure the measurement window is
	  sized around; the 6 s default is that plus margin. Nothing shorter
	  has been tried on hardware, because there is no hardware -- do not
	  lower the floor on the strength of the filter time constant alone.

config RING_HR_CONTINUOUS
	bool "Keep the PPG running continuously (heart-rate test builds only)"
	default n
	help
	  Opens a measurement window at boot and never closes it, so heart
	  rate is computed and notified over the standard Heart Rate Service
	  once a second for as long as the board is up. Nothing has to pair
	  or send an opcode: any HRS client that connects sees live BPM.

	  Exists to test the optical path and the beat detector against a
	  reference on a bench. It ignores the duty cycle, the no-finger
	  timeout and the stillness timeout, so green LED1 and the 5 V boost
	  run the whole time -- never ship it. Built with hr-test.conf into
	  build-hrtest, so it cannot be left where a production image is
	  expected.

config RING_PPG_ON_DEMAND
	bool "Measure only when the app asks -- no automatic windows"
	default n
	help
	  Drops the duty cycle: no scheduled window every minute, no window
	  on a motion wake. Green LED1 and the 5 V boost come on only when
	  the app asks -- PPG streaming (opcode 0x01), which holds a window
	  open for as long as it runs, or measure-now (opcode 0x03), which
	  opens one 25 s window. A connected app can do everything it could
	  before; the ring just never decides to measure on its own.

	  For demos and bench sessions where the ring is carried around all
	  day and should only light up when someone is watching. It also
	  means no heart rate unless an app is connected and asking, so it
	  is not a shipping configuration. Built by .\build.ps1 -Demo with
	  demo.conf, which also turns the sleep wear check off.

config RING_CHARGE_LED
	bool "Hold the red LED on for as long as the charger is attached"
	default n
	help
	  Without this, attaching a charger flashes LED3 red for 3 s and then
	  leaves the optics dark. With it, red stays on until the charger is
	  removed, and the ring then goes back to whatever it was doing --
	  in an HR test build, green heart-rate measurement.

	  It is not free: the indicator holds the 5 V boost up and averages
	  roughly 2 mA of LED current (~3 mA at the cell) against a 20 mA
	  charge current, so a charge takes on the order of 15 % longer.
	  Off by default for that reason; hr-test.conf turns it on.

config RING_MEASURE_PERIOD_S
	int "Seconds between automatic measurement windows"
	default 60
	range 15 3600
	help
	  Each window lights green LED1 for 25 s, and the LED plus the 5 V
	  boost are the dominant draw on this board. 60 s is 25 % duty;
	  daily.conf uses 600 s (2.5 %) so a ring worn all day lasts the
	  day. The app can still change it at runtime with opcode 0x04.

config RING_HISTORY_EPOCH_S
	int "Seconds summarised by each on-ring history record"
	default 300
	range 60 3600
	help
	  The ring writes one 32-byte summary to flash every epoch -- heart
	  rate (mean, min, max), RMSSD, spot readings, steps, temperature,
	  motion, hot flashes, battery and worn/charging/asleep/workout flags
	  -- whether or not a phone is connected, and the app downloads them
	  later (control opcode 0x0E). See src/history.h and
	  APP_INTEGRATION.md section 16.

	  The partition holds 5,504 records, so capacity is this times
	  5,504: 19 days at the default 300 s, 3.8 days at 60 s. Shorter
	  epochs also wear the flash faster, though even 60 s cycles each
	  page only about twice a week.

config RING_PPG_LED_PA
	int "Green LED drive code for heart-rate windows (0.12 mA per LSB)"
	default 255
	range 1 255
	help
	  0xFF (255) is ~30.6 mA, 0x80 (128) ~15.4 mA, in the 31 mA range.
	  Together with RING_PPG_AVG_LOG2 this sets the LED energy per
	  sample, which dominates the ring's power budget while it measures
	  (POWER.md): the MAXM86161 draws ~600 uA from VLED at 100 sps with
	  0xFF and one pulse a sample, and scales with both settings.

	  255 is what every test build ran since v0.6.2. prod.conf sets 128.
	  Opcode 0x10 overrides it at run time in test builds.

config RING_PPG_AVG_LOG2
	int "On-chip averaging for heart-rate windows: 2^n pulses a sample"
	default 2
	range 0 2
	help
	  0, 1, 2 = average 1, 2, 4 pulses; output stays 100 sps. Each step
	  doubles the LED energy. 2 (4x) is what every test build ran since
	  v0.6.2; prod.conf sets 1 (2x).

	  Why production is not at 4x: on 2026-10-08's 30 s recordings, the
	  cheapest setting (15 mA, no averaging) scored as well as 31 mA with
	  4x once the detector despikes -- at an eighth of the LED energy --
	  but 30 s per setting cannot separate them. Settle it with
	  tools/ppg_sweep.py --ab against a WHOOP.

config RING_IMU_LOW_POWER
	bool "Run the accelerometer in low-power mode at 60 Hz"
	default n
	help
	  The accelerometer runs all day -- wake-on-motion and the step
	  counter need it -- and every build so far ran it in high-performance
	  mode at 120 Hz: ~190 uA by the LSM6DSV datasheet, several mAh a day
	  on its own. Low-power mode with 4-sample averaging at 60 Hz is ~20
	  uA, with somewhat more noise -- a few mg, against the ~80-100 mg
	  swings that wake the ring and count a step.

	  NOT YET VERIFIED ON HARDWARE. The register value comes from ST's
	  LSM6DSV16BX driver, but nobody has checked that wake-on-motion still
	  fires and steps still count in this mode on the ring. Off in the test
	  builds; prod.conf turns it on, and the first prod flash must confirm
	  both (ROADMAP.md).

config RING_DIAG_OPCODES
	bool "Accept diagnostic control opcodes 0x10 (PPG tune) and 0x11 (TX power)"
	default y
	help
	  Lets tools/ppg_sweep.py change the PPG front end and the radio's
	  transmit power over BLE without a reflash. On for every test build,
	  as it was in v0.6.2.

	  Off in production (prod.conf): they are tuning instruments, not
	  settings, and 0x11 is suspected of one reboot (2026-10-08) that was
	  never cleared. With this off both opcodes are refused with ATT 0x13
	  like any unknown opcode.

config RING_GSR_STATUS
	bool "Sample GSR every 30 s for the status packet"
	default y
	help
	  Powers the GSR front end and waits its 800 ms settle every 30 s to
	  fill gsr_mv in the Status characteristic. On for every test build,
	  as it was in v0.6.2.

	  Off in production (prod.conf), for two reasons learned in testing:
	  the front end cannot resolve skin conductance on this board (gain
	  about 10x too low, noise larger than the signal), and the 800 ms
	  settle blocks the main loop, so the step counter misses a stride
	  every 30 s of walking. gsr_mv then reads -1 ("not measured"). The
	  raw GSR stream (opcode 0x08) still works for diagnostics.

config RING_ADV_SLOW_AFTER_S
	int "Seconds of fast advertising before slowing to ~1 s (0 = never)"
	default 0
	range 0 3600
	help
	  The ring advertises every 30-60 ms so a phone finds it at once. Kept
	  up all day while nothing is connected, that is roughly 20 radio
	  events a second, likely several mAh a day at +8 dBm -- a large share
	  of a battery this small. With this set, advertising
	  drops to 1-1.2 s once it has run fast for this long after boot or
	  a disconnect. A phone still finds the ring within about a second;
	  it just stops paying for instant discovery nobody is waiting on.

	  0 (the default) keeps the fast advertising every test build had.
	  prod.conf sets 30.

config RING_CONN_PARAM_MGMT
	bool "Ask the central for an idle or fast connection interval"
	default n
	help
	  Without this the ring keeps whatever interval the phone or PC
	  chose, typically 15-30 ms, for the whole connection. With it the
	  ring asks for ~100 ms with latency 4 while idle, waking about every
	  half second, and 15-30 ms while a raw stream or a history sync
	  needs the throughput. See ble_service() in ble.c.

	  Off by default, as every test build was. prod.conf turns it on.

config RING_HRS_OPEN
	bool "Leave the standard Heart Rate Service readable without pairing"
	default y
	depends on BT_HRS
	help
	  Heart rate is a biometric, and by default this firmware broadcasts
	  it over the standard Heart Rate Service (0x180D) with no encryption
	  required: anyone in range who connects can read it. That is a
	  deliberate trade, not an oversight.

	  Keep it y and any off-the-shelf HR app -- a watch face, a cycling
	  computer, a generic BLE HRM client -- pairs with nothing and just
	  works, which is the whole point of implementing the SIG service
	  instead of only the custom one. The cost is that resting heart rate
	  leaks to anybody within a few metres.

	  Set it to n and the HRS characteristics require an encrypted link,
	  so only a bonded phone can read heart rate. Generic clients that do
	  not pair will see the service, fail to subscribe, and show nothing.
	  The Ring Service status packet has always been encrypted and carries
	  hr_x10, so a first-party app loses nothing either way.

	  This does not touch the Battery Service, which stays open.

config RING_SPOT_NIGHT_MIN
	int "Minutes between night spot readings (0 = off)"
	default 0
	range 0 720
	help
	  A spot reading is a long green PPG window -- RING_SPOT_WINDOW_S --
	  taken for a clean resting heart rate and RMSSD rather than a quick
	  heart rate. Night readings start 15 min after sleep onset and
	  repeat at this interval while asleep. They are what the app's
	  burnout trend is built on. See src/spot.h.

	  Off by default, as every test build was. prod.conf sets 60. The
	  app can change it at run time (opcode 0x12, RAM only). 1-9 are
	  refused there, and raised to 10 here (spot_init()): back-to-back
	  readings would keep the LED on all night.

config RING_SPOT_DAY_MIN
	int "Minutes between daytime spot readings (0 = off)"
	default 0
	range 0 720
	help
	  Daytime spot readings: awake, worn, and the accelerometer still
	  for 3 minutes, at most once per this interval. The app's stress
	  readings come from these, against the night baseline.

	  Off by default. prod.conf sets 60.

config RING_SPOT_WINDOW_S
	int "Length of a spot reading, seconds"
	default 180
	range 60 600
	help
	  Long enough for the beat detector to prime (~10 s) and then
	  collect well over a hundred consecutive beats at a resting heart
	  rate; the reading's RMSSD is over all of them (hrv_session). Each reading at the default costs about seven
	  25 s heart-rate windows' worth of LED.

config RING_HOT_FLASH_MODE
	int "Hot-flash detection: 0 off, 1 while asleep, 2 always"
	default 0
	range 0 2
	help
	  Watches the ring temperature for the quick rise of a hot flash --
	  see src/thermo.h for the algorithm and its caveats. The
	  temperature itself is logged in every build regardless; this only
	  decides whether events are detected, counted, flagged in the
	  history and followed by a spot reading.

	  Off by default. prod.conf sets 1: night sweats are the case the
	  temperature can see cleanly, a still hand under a duvet. The app
	  can change it (opcode 0x13).

config RING_HOT_FLASH_RISE_C10
	int "Hot-flash onset threshold, tenths of a degree C"
	default 10
	range 3 50
	help
	  How far above its settled baseline the ring temperature has to
	  climb, three quarters of it within 10 minutes. 1.0 C is a
	  starting point, not a fit: the nRF die reads the finger through
	  the ring, attenuated, and nobody has worn this board through a
	  hot flash yet.

# Selecting a choice entry provokes a Kconfig warning, so steer the choice
# instead. This file is KCONFIG_ROOT and sources Kconfig.zephyr at the end,
# so this default is evaluated before the upstream one in
# subsys/bluetooth/services/Kconfig.hrs.
choice BT_HRS_DEFAULT_PERM
	default BT_HRS_DEFAULT_PERM_RW_ENCRYPT if BT_HRS && !RING_HRS_OPEN
endchoice

endmenu

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
